[[17592186134396 :question/problem "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y = β_{0} + β_{2}x^{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}β_{0} \\\\ β_{2}\\end{bmatrix} = (X^{T}X)^{-1}X^{T}y$,\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?"] [17592186134396 :question/answer-fields 17592186132385] [17592186162615 :question/problem "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x) = x_{1}^{2} + 5x_{3}^{2} - 2x_{1}x_{2} - 4x_{1}x_{3} + 4x_{2}x_{3}$ to the canonical form given below.\n\n$y_{1}^{2} - y_{2}^{2} + y_{3}^{2}, {y_{1} = x_{1} - x_{2} - 2x_{3}; y_{2} = 0x_{2}; y_{3} = x_{3}$\n\nFrom top to bottom, what is missing from the blank spaces?"] [17592186162615 :question/answer-fields 17592186160952] [17592186231298 :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n$y''+2y=f(x), \\qquad y'(0)=0,\\quad y'(1)=-5$\nwhere $f(x)$ is $2$-periodic and even. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution $y_p$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution $y_p$ satisfies $y_p'(0) = 0$ and $y_p'(1) = 0$.*\n$\\frac{1}{xxx}?\\frac{1}{xxx}$"] [17592186231298 :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as\n\n$$\ny=y_c+y_p\n$$\n\n, where $y_c$ solves the homogeneous equation and $y_p$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\ny''+2y=0\n$$\n\nIts general solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x)=A\\cos(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\sin(\\sqrt{2}x)\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\ny'(x)=-A\\sqrt{2}\\sin(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\sqrt{2}\\cos(\\sqrt{2}x)+y_p'(x)\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since $y_p'(0)=0$ and $y_p'(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny'(0)=y_c'(0)\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y'(1)=y_c'(1)\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y'(0)=0{:}$\n$y^{′}(0)|= 0 + B\\sqrt{2}(1) + y_{p}^{′}(0); 0|= B\\sqrt{2} + 0; B|= 0$\nSo, $y_c(x)=A\\cos(\\sqrt{2}x)$.\n- Use $y'(1)=-5$ with $B=0{:}$\n$y^{′}(1)|=-A\\sqrt{2}\\sin (\\sqrt{2}) + y_{p}^{′}(1); - 5|=-A\\sqrt{2}\\sin (\\sqrt{2}) + 0; A|= (5)/(\\sqrt{2}\\sin (\\sqrt{2}))$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)\\sim \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt gray]{\\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\sin(\\sqrt{2})}\\cos(\\sqrt{2}x)} +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n\\cos(n\\pi x)\n$$"] [17592186231302 :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n$y''+8y=f(x)$\non $[-3,3]$, where $f$ is $6$-periodic and even. What Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution $y_p$?"] [17592186231302 :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $6$-periodic on $[-3,3]$, we have $L=3$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no $y'$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for $y_p$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} a_n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$"] [17592186231580 :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n$y^{″} + 5y = f(x), y^{′}(0) = 0, y^{′}(1) = 4$\nwhere $f(x)$ is $2$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution $y_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution $y_{p}$ satisfies $y_{p}^{′}(0) = 0$ and $y_{p}^{′}(1) = 0$.*\n\n$- (4)/(\\sqrt{5}\\sin (\\sqrt{5}))\\cos (\\sqrt{5}x)$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186231580 :question/answer-fields 17592186230318] [17592186221947 :question/problem "Given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}L{\\sin ωt}|= \\frac{ω}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, & s > 0, \\\\ L{\\cos ωt}|= \\frac{s}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, & s > 0\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind $L{u(t - 1)\\cos (3πt)}$, where $u(t)$ is the unit step function."] [17592186221947 :question/answer-fields 17592186220249] [17592186222040 :question/problem "Find $L{u(t - 2)(t - 2)^{2}}$, where $u(t)$ is the unit step function."] [17592186222040 :question/answer-fields 17592186220900] [17592186134306 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S = Span{v, w}$, where\n$v = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, w = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${v, w}$ is orthogonal.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186134306 :question/answer-fields 17592186131845] [17592186176402 :question/problem "![](images/33/3358dd177a848ea569fdeab6e39f4be268cc7325bb01c788e9bfe2ea2145d1eb.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the hemisphere $x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} = 25$ for $y \\ge 0$, shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as"] [17592186176402 :question/answer-fields 17592186174640] [17592186229664 :question/problem "Consider the homogeneous system $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$, where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix $Φ_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n$Φ_{0}^{′}(t) = AΦ_{0}(t), Φ_{0}(0) = I_{n}$.\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x(0)$ is presented below.\n\n> $L1$: Let $Φ_{0}(t) = e^{At}$\n> $L2$: $x(t) = Φ_{0}(t)c$ for some constant vector $c$\n> $L3$: $x(0) = Φ_{0}(0)c$\n> $L4$: $Φ_{0}(0) = I_{n}$\n> $L5$: $x(0) = c$\n> $L6$: $x(t) = e^{At}x(0)$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ follows from $L1$ by the\n{{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L3$ follows from $L2$ by\n{{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L6$ follows from line $L2$ by substitution from lines {{field-3}}."] [17592186229664 :question/answer-fields 17592186228144] [17592186229664 :question/answer-fields 17592186228149] [17592186229664 :question/answer-fields 17592186228154] [17592186222035 :question/problem "$f(x)∼\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}\\cos (\\frac{nπ}{3}x) + b_{n}\\sin (\\frac{nπ}{3}x)$\nLet $F(x) = x$ for $x ∈ [- 3, 3)$. The Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of $a_{0}$.$a_{0} =$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186222035 :question/answer-fields 17592186220865] [17592186161908 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & -\\frac{3}{5} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A$, find the pseudoinverse $A^+$."] [17592186161908 :question/worked-solution "If\n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V ^T\n$$\n\nis a singular value decomposition of $A$, then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV \\, \\Sigma^+ \\, U^T\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Sigma^+$ is the pseudoinverse of $\\Sigma$.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{+} &= VΣ^{+}U^{T} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186161909 :question/problem "For the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ find the pseudoinverse $A^+$."] [17592186161909 :question/worked-solution "For an \"almost\" zero $m \\times n$ matrix with non-zero entries\n\n$$\n\\sigma_1, \\: \\sigma_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: \\sigma_r\n$$\n\non the main diagonal, the corresponding pseudoinverse is the \"almost\" zero $n \\times m$ matrix with entries\n\n$$\n{\\sigma_1^{-1}}, \\, {\\sigma_2^{-1}}, \\, \\ldots, \\, {\\sigma_r^{-1}}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{+} &= \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}^{+} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}6^{-1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4^{-1} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186161910 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U_r \\Sigma_r V_r^T$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A$, find the pseudoinverse $A^+$."] [17592186161910 :question/worked-solution "If\n\n$$\nA=U_r \\Sigma_r V_r ^T\n$$\n\nis a reduced singular value decomposition of $A$, then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV_r \\, \\Sigma_r^+ \\, U_r^T\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Sigma_r^+$ is the pseudoinverse of $\\Sigma_r$.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{+} &= V_{r}Σ_{r}^{+}U_{r}^{T} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[1] \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot [1] \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 16 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186162354 :question/problem "$U_{r} = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}, Σ_{r} = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}, V_{r} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A = U_{r}Σ_{r}V_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A$, find the pseudoinverse $A^{+}$."] [17592186162354 :question/answer-fields 17592186159296] [17592186162365 :question/problem "$U_{r} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}, Σ_{r} = [\\sqrt{10}], V_{r} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A = U_{r}Σ_{r}V_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A$, find the pseudoinverse $A^{+}$."] [17592186162365 :question/answer-fields 17592186159362] [17592186231303 :question/problem "![](images/fa/fae88c8da0b50da2bc025cbac0dbc376be3b67b69b59823746873c6e7d102dcc.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*"] [17592186231303 :question/worked-solution "The circuit is a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\cos(4t)\\,\\textrm{V}$, a resistor of resistance $30\\,\\Omega$, a capacitor of capacitance $0.002\\,\\textrm{F}$, and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\textrm{H}$.\n\nThe current $I$, measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$, measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,\\Omega$. So,\n$V_R = 30I$.\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$. Therefore,\n$V_C=\\dfrac{q}{C}=\\dfrac{q}{0.002}=500q$.\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\textrm{H}$. Hence,\n$V_L = 5\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}$.\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n6\\cos(4t)=30I+500q+5\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t$, the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(6\\cos (4t)) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(30I + 500q + 5\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\\n- 24\\sin (4t) &= 30\\frac{dI}{dt} + 500\\frac{dq}{dt} + 5\\frac{d^{2}I}{dt^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+100I=-\\dfrac{24}{5}\\sin(4t)\n$$"] [17592186231304 :question/problem "![](images/08/085fe02b43301629af58379289fc9d1c49c90e71a83e0dd33f491ecf4b21bfc8.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\dfrac25\\,\\textrm{C}$ and the initial current is $\\dfrac34\\,\\textrm{A}$.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*"] [17592186231304 :question/worked-solution "The circuit is a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\textrm{V}$, a resistor of resistance $10\\,\\Omega$, a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\textrm{F}$, and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\textrm{H}$.\n\nThe current $I$, measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$, measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,\\Omega$. So,\n$V_R = 10I$.\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$. Therefore,\n$V_C=\\dfrac{q}{C}=\\dfrac{q}{0.25}=4q$.\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\textrm{H}$. Hence,\n$V_L = 4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}$.\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n9=10I+4q+4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t$, the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(9) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(10I + 4q + 4\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\\n0 &= 10\\frac{dI}{dt} + 4\\frac{dq}{dt} + 4\\frac{d^{2}I}{dt^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+\\dfrac52\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+I=0\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form\n\n$$\nI=e^{\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nwe obtain two solutions, namely, $I=e^{-2t}$ and\n\n$$\nI=e^{-t/2}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=Ae^{-2t}+Be^{-t/2}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac25\\,\\textrm{C}\n$$\n\non the capacitor and an initial current of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac34\\,\\textrm{A}\n$$\n\nNow, solving the Kirchhoff's law equation for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}=\\dfrac94-\\dfrac52I-q\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions\n\n$$\nI(0)=\\dfrac34\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI^{′}(0) &= \\frac{9}{4} - \\frac{5}{2}I(0) - q(0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{4} - \\frac{5}{2} \\cdot \\frac{3}{4} - \\frac{2}{5} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{40}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B$.\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=\\dfrac34$ into our general solution gives\n$\\frac{3}{4}|= Ae^{-2(0)} + Be^{-0/2}; \\frac{3}{4}|= A + B$.\n- Differentiating $I$, we get\n$I'=-2Ae^{-2t}-\\dfrac12Be^{-t/2}$.\nSubstituting in $I'(0)=-\\dfrac1{40}$ gives\n$-\\frac{1}{40}|=-2Ae^{-2(0)} - \\frac{1}{2}Be^{-0/2}; - \\frac{1}{40}|=-2A - \\frac{1}{2}B$.\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{3}{4} = A + B; - \\frac{1}{40} =-2A - \\frac{1}{2}B\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives\n\n$$\nA=-\\dfrac{7}{30}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nB=\\dfrac{59}{60}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=-\\dfrac{7}{30}e^{-2t}+\\dfrac{59}{60}e^{-t/2}\n$$"] [17592186231305 :question/problem "![](images/32/323324bc8d0ab017d30bc2a37e5e5e66784d64da6309fc131d56cb576ed2b003.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*"] [17592186231305 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\cos(2t)\\,\\textrm{V}$, a resistor of resistance $8\\,\\Omega$, a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\textrm{F}$, and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\textrm{H}$.\n\nThe current $I$, measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$, measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=8\\,\\Omega$. So,\n$V_R = 8I$.\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$. Therefore,\n$V_C = \\dfrac{q}{C} = \\dfrac{q}{0.25} = 4q$.\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\textrm{H}$. Hence,\n$V_L = 4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}$.\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n6\\cos(2t)=8I+4q+4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t$, the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(6\\cos (2t)) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(8I + 4q + 4\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\\n- 12\\sin (2t) &= 8\\frac{dI}{dt} + 4\\frac{dq}{dt} + 4\\frac{d^{2}I}{dt^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+2\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+I=-3\\sin(2t)\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $I_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 2\\, \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t}+ I_c=0$.\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n$I_c(t)=Ae^{-t}+Bte^{-t}$,\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t$. So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t$, i.e.,\n$I_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(2t)+\\beta\\sin(2t)$.\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, we substitute this, as well as\n$\\frac{dI_{p}}{dt}|=-2α\\sin (2t) + 2β\\cos (2t),; \\frac{d^{2}I_{p}}{dt^{2}}|=-4α\\cos (2t) - 4β\\sin (2t)$,\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n$(-4α\\cos (2t) - 4β\\sin (2t)) + 2(-2α\\sin (2t) + 2β\\cos (2t)); + (α\\cos (2t) + β\\sin (2t))|=-3\\sin (2t); (-3α + 4β)\\cos (2t) + (-4α - 3β)\\sin (2t)|=-3\\sin (2t)$\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n${-3α + 4β = 0\\begin{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients of\\cos (2t)) \\\\ -4α - 3β =-3\\end{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients of\\sin (2t))$\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=\\dfrac{12}{25}$ and $\\beta=\\dfrac{9}{25}$. Therefore, the particular solution is\n$I_p(t)=\\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t)+\\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t)$.\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t) = I_c(t) + I_p(t) = Ae^{-t} + Bte^{-t} + \\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t)\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c(t) = Ae^{-t} + Bte^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty$.\n- The function $I_p(t) = \\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty$, we have\n\n$$\nI(t)\\approx I_p(t) = \\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t)\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{12}{25}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{9}{25}\\right)^2} = \\dfrac35\n$$"] [17592186231583 :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12V$, a resistance of $40Ω$, a capacitance of $0.005F$, and an inductance of $L = 20H$. Find the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) in the circuit at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*"] [17592186231583 :question/answer-fields 17592186230334] [17592186231584 :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb13301f8706ec829441c4a56b7c170e8d43e6ebfd0b8718dfab9aeb823a6b53.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*"] [17592186231584 :question/answer-fields 17592186230340] [17592186231589 :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$, in coulombs, and current $I(t)$, in amperes, in a series RCL circuit are related by the equation\n\n$\\frac{dI}{dt} + 5I + 6q = 12$.\n\nFind an equation for the current in the circuit, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\frac{9}{20}C$ and the initial current is $\\frac{9}{10}A$."] [17592186231589 :question/answer-fields 17592186230370] [17592186231590 :question/problem "![](images/d3/d399a25c2a73883560aade5946007b21bf0b853e5d3ca262469df9b75e5843cd.png)\n\nConsider the RCL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ in the circuit at time $t$ seconds is given by\n$I(t) = Ae^{-5t/2} + Be^{-t} + \\frac{P}{41}\\cos (2t) + \\frac{Q}{41}\\sin (2t)$,\nwhere $A$, $B$, $P$, and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P + Q$.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*"] [17592186231590 :question/answer-fields 17592186230376] [17592186179432 :question/problem "Construct the vector field that has the stream function $\\psi(x,y) = e^{x} \\cos{y}$."] [17592186179432 :question/worked-solution "A function $\\psi(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F= P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nif the following equations hold:\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y},\\qquad Q = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives of the function $\\psi(x,y)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP &= \\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(e^{x}\\cos y) \\\\\n&=-e^{x}\\sin y, \\\\\nQ &= -\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(e^{x}\\cos y) \\\\\n&=-e^{x}\\cos y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field with the stream function\n\n$$\n\\psi(x,y) = e^{x} \\cos{y}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(x, y) &= Pi + Qj \\\\\n&=-e^{x}\\sin yi - e^{x}\\cos yj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179433 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = x^4y\\,\\mathbf{i} - 2x^3y^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$\n2. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\sin x \\sin y\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\cos x \\cos y\\,\\mathbf{j}$\n3. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = 4x^2y\\,\\mathbf{i} -4x^2y^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$"] [17592186179433 :question/worked-solution "A vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nwith a simply connected domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$ has a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field I has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂x} = 4x^{3}y, \\frac{∂Q}{∂y}|=-4x^{3}y$.\nTherefore,\n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 4x^3y + (-4x^3y) = 0$\n\n- Vector field II has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂x} = \\cos x\\sin y, \\frac{∂Q}{∂y}|=-\\cos x\\sin y$.\nTherefore,\n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = \\cos x \\sin y + (-\\cos x \\sin y) = 0$.\n- Vector field III doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂x} = 8xy, \\frac{∂Q}{∂y}|=-8x^{2}y$.\nTherefore,\n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 8xy + (-8x^2y) \\neq 0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186179441 :question/problem "Determine the equation of the streamline that passes through the point $P(-1,1)$ for the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (x^3-3xy^2)\\,\\mathbf{i} + (y^3-3x^2y)\\,\\mathbf{j}$."] [17592186179441 :question/worked-solution "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point.\n\nIf $\\mathbf F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nA vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nwith a simply connected domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$ has a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (x^3-3xy^2)\\,\\mathbf{i} + (y^3-3x^2y)\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nis a vector field with simply connected domain\n\n$$\nD=\\mathbb{R}^2\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = x^3-3xy^2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y) = y^3 - 3x^2y\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x} = 3x^{2} - 3y^{2}, \\frac{∂Q}{∂y} &= 3y^{2} - 3x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathbf F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = (3x^2-3y^2) + (3y^2 - 3x^2) = 0\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $\\psi(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F= P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nif\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}, \\qquad Q = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y} = x^3-3xy^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x} = -y^3+3x^2y\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\psi(x,y) = x^3y - xy^3 + f(x), \\qquad \\psi(x,y) = -xy^3 + x^3y + g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\psi(x,y) = x^3y - xy^3\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curve of the stream function\n\n$$\n\\psi(x,y) = x^3y - xy^3\n$$\n\nthat passes through $P$.\nFirst, we find the value of the stream function at $P{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nψ(-1, 1) &= (-1)^{3}(1) - (-1)(1)^{3} \\\\\n&=-1 - (-1) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the streamline that passes through $P(-1,1)$ is the $0$-level curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nψ(x, y) &= 0 \\\\\nx^{3}y - xy^{3} &= 0 \\\\\nxy(x^{2} - y^{2}) &= 0 \\\\\nxy(x + y)(x - y) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our possible streamlines are $x=0$, $y=0$, $y=-x$, or $y=x$. The streamline that passes through $P(-1,1)$ is $y=-x$.\nThe vector field and the streamline through the point $P$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/29/29512dff5cf2dfe6dbebc0fadeee51ee3043b0161dc1729cc89877e6b39512af.png)"] [17592186179443 :question/problem "Find a stream function for the vector field $\\mathbf F$, given by\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = 2y\\,\\mathbf{i} +2x\\,\\mathbf{j}$."] [17592186179443 :question/worked-solution "A vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nwith a simply connected domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$ has a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = 2y\\,\\mathbf{i} +2x\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nis a vector field with simply connected domain\n\n$$\nD=\\mathbb{R}^2\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = 2y\n$$\n\nand $Q(x,y) =2x$. Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x} = 0, \\frac{∂Q}{∂y} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathbf F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0 + 0 = 0\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $\\psi(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F= P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nif\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}, \\qquad Q = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y} = 2y, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x} = -2x\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\psi(x,y) = y^2 + f(x), \\qquad \\psi(x,y) = -x^2+ g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting\n\n$$\nf(x)=-x^2\n$$\n\nand $g(y)=y^2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\psi(x,y) = y^2-x^2\n$$"] [17592186180090 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\n1. $F(x, y) = yi - xj$\n2. $F(x, y) = xyi + y^{2}j$\n3. $F(x, y) = 2xi - yj$"] [17592186180090 :question/answer-fields 17592186178179] [17592186180097 :question/problem "Determine the equation of the streamline that passes through the point $P(-1,-1)$ for the vector field $F(x, y) = x^{2}i - 2xyj$."] [17592186180097 :question/answer-fields 17592186178221] [17592186083847 :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n$r = ⟨8t^{2}, 45⟩, t > 0$.\nCalculate the velocity vector $v$ at time $t = 3$."] [17592186083847 :question/answer-fields 17592186081983] [17592186134138 :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t) = (t^{2} - t)i + (t - t^{3})j + (2t^{2} - 2t)k$ for $t ∈ [0, 1)$.\nGiven that $F(x, y, z) = yi + xk$, evaluate the integral\n$∬_(S)curlF \\cdot dS$."] [17592186134138 :question/answer-fields 17592186130954] [17592186129507 :question/problem "Find the value of the double sum\n\n$∑_(i = 1)^(10)∑_(j = 1)^(20)(a_{i} - 1)(b_{j} + 1)$\n\ngiven that\n\n$∑_(i = 1)^(10)a_{i} = 12, ∑_(j = 1)^(20)b_{j} = 5$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186129507 :question/answer-fields 17592186126512] [17592186125061 :question/problem "If $f(x, y) = \\cos x + y$, use the differential $df$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x, y)$ from $(4π, 8)$ to $(13, 8.5)$."] [17592186125061 :question/answer-fields 17592186122936] [17592186198977 :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/4}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/4}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\tan y}f(x, y, z)dxdzdy$.\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $y$, then $x$, and then $z$."] [17592186198977 :question/answer-fields 17592186197702] [17592186198051 :question/problem "![](images/f9/f9b1b286210d694cd3786fe38190216da503bfa3223114f86c8706f810313e33.png)\n\nThe curve $C_1$ has endpoints $A(5,3)$ and $B(1,3)$. Calculate the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\sqrt{y^3}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{(5-x)^3}\\,\\mathbf{j}$\nthrough $C_1$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_1$, as shown above.\n\n*Hint: You're given that $\\mathbf F$ is source-free.*"] [17592186198051 :question/worked-solution "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nis a vector field with simply connected domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$, then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field.\n- $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0$.\n- The flux $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s}$ along any closed curve $C \\in D$ is zero.\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves $C_1, C_2 \\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n$\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = \\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s$.\n- There exists a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ for $\\mathbf{F}$.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\sqrt{y^3}\\right) + \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\sqrt{(5-x)^3}\\right) = 0+0 = 0\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $\\mathbf{F}$ is source-free, we know the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ is independent of the path. In particular,\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\,\\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\,\\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere $C_2$ is the straight line from $A(5,3)$ to $B(1,3)$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2$ is its unit normal, shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/2a/2aeb26e58251923821d4058f886eada6a29b473ea70714b32e98c5338ddd0b28.png)\n\nNotice that both $\\mathbf n_1$ and $\\mathbf n_2$ are $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C_2$, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\,\\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_2} P\\,\\textrm{d}y - Q\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t \\in [a,b]$, and\n- the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_2$ *must* be a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $\\mathbf{T}(t)$.\n\nParametrizing $C_2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= 5i + 3j + t \\cdot [(i + 3j) - (5i + 3j)] \\\\\n&= (5 - 4t)i + 3j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,1]$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x, y) &= \\sqrt{y^{3}} = \\sqrt{(3)^{3}} = 3\\sqrt{3}, \\\\\nQ(x, y) &= \\sqrt{(5 - x)^{3}} = \\sqrt{(5 - (5 - 4t))^{3}} = 8\\sqrt{t^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(5 - 4t) =-4, \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(3) = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_(C_{2})F \\cdot n_{2}ds &= \\int_(C_{2})Pdy - Qdx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(P \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dt} - Q \\cdot \\frac{dx}{dt})dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(3\\sqrt{3}) \\cdot (0) - (8\\sqrt{t^{3}}) \\cdot (-4)dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}32t^{3/2}dt \\\\\n&= [\\frac{64t^{5/2}}{5}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= ((64(1)^{5/2})/(5)) - (((64(0)^{5/2})/(5)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{64}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1\\, \\textrm{d}s = \\dfrac{64}{5}\n$$"] [17592186198248 :question/problem "Given the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = 4y(x^2+y^2)\\,\\mathbf{i} - 4x(x^2+y^2)\\,\\mathbf{j}$, which of the following statements must be true?\n\n1. $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F= 0$\n2. $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field\n3. For two arbitrary curves $C_1$ and $C_2$, we have $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s \\neq \\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s$."] [17592186198248 :question/worked-solution "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nis a vector field with simply connected domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$, then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field.\n- $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0$.\n- The flux $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s}$ along any closed curve $C \\in D$ is zero.\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves $C_1,C_2 \\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n$\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = \\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s$.\n- There exists a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ for $\\mathbf{F}$.\n\nNote that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ is a vector field with the simply connected domain\n\n$$\nD=\\mathbb{R}^2\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = 4y(x^2+y^2),\\qquad Q(x,y) =-4x(x^2+y^2)\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x} = 8xy, \\frac{∂Q}{∂y} &= -8xy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 8xy + (-8xy) = 0$.\n- Statement II is true. This follows from statement I.\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf F$ is source-free, we have that $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = \\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s$ for all curves *with the same start and endpoints*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186092713 :question/problem "Find the **polar** coordinates of the point where the polar curve $r = 4$ intersects the negative $x$-axis."] [17592186092713 :question/answer-fields 17592186090448] [17592186198024 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ enclosed in the first quadrant of the $xy$-plane between the curves\n$y=x, \\qquad y=7x, \\qquad x=\\dfrac{3}{y^2}, \\qquad x=\\dfrac{5}{y^2}$.\n\nA transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, where\n\n$\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v))$,\n\nmaps a rectangular region $\\Delta$ with sides parallel to the $u$- and $v$-axes from the $uv$-plane onto $D$ in the $xy$-plane. Given that\n$\\mathbf{T}: (u,v) \\to \\Big(\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{v}{u^2}}, \\: \\bbox[5pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} \\, \\Big)$,\nwhat could be the missing expression?"] [17592186198024 :question/worked-solution "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔ &= {(u, v): a \\le u \\le b, c \\le v \\le d} \\\\\n&= {(u, v): a \\le u(x, y) \\le b, c \\le v(x, y) \\le d}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}u(x,y)} = a, \\qquad {\\color{blue}u(x,y)} = b, \\qquad {\\color{red}v(x,y)} = c, \\qquad {\\color{red}v(x,y)} = d\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D$.\n\nRewriting the equations\n\n$$\ny=x, \\qquad y=7x, \\qquad x=\\dfrac{3}{y^2}, \\qquad x=\\dfrac{5}{y^2}\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{y}{x} = 1, \\frac{y}{x} = 7, xy^{2} = 3, xy^{2} = 5\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put\n\n$$\nu(x,y)={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{y}{x}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nv(x,y)={\\color{red}xy^2}\n$$\n\nwe can define the transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}: (x,y) \\to \\left(\\, {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{y}{x}}\\,, \\: \\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{\\color{red}xy^2} \\right)\n$$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{T}$, we should express $x$ and $y$ in terms of $u$ and $v$. Recall that we have\n\n$$\n{u = \\frac{y}{x}; v = xy^{2}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, $y=ux$. Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nv = u^2x^3 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x^3 = \\dfrac{v}{u^2} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x = \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{v}{u^2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\nx = \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{v}{u^2}}\n$$\n\nback into $y=ux$, we obtain\n\n$$\ny =u\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{v}{u^2}} = \\sqrt[3]{uv}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ can be given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: (u,v) \\to \\left(\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{v}{u^2}}, \\, \\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{\\sqrt[3]{uv}} \\right)\n$$"] [17592186198242 :question/problem "Consider the region $D$, given by\n$D = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 2 \\leq \\sqrt{xy} \\leq 4, \\: 2x \\leq y \\leq 3x \\big\\}$\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T$, given by\n$\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v))$,\nwhere $u=\\dfrac{y}{x}$ and $v=xy$. Find the representation of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane."] [17592186198242 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)$ is the image of $D$ under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$.\n\nLet's find the image of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane. To do that, we write the region $D$ in terms of $u$ and $v$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{-1}(D) &= {(u, v): 2 \\le \\sqrt{xy} \\le 4, 2x \\le y \\le 3x} \\\\\n&= {(u, v): 2^{2} \\le (\\sqrt{xy})^{2} \\le 4^{2}, 2 \\le \\frac{y}{x} \\le 3} \\\\\n&= {(u, v): 4 \\le xy \\le 16, 2 \\le \\frac{y}{x} \\le 3} \\\\\n&= {(u, v): 4 \\le v \\le 16, 2 \\le u \\le 3} \\\\\n&= {(u, v): 2 \\le u \\le 3, 4 \\le v \\le 16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since\n\n$$\n2 \\leq \\sqrt{xy}\\leq 4\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\nx \\neq 0\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nu=\\dfrac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nis well-defined."] [17592186198243 :question/problem "Let $D$ be the finite region bounded by the curve\n$(x+y)^2 + y = 3$ and the line $y=-1$.\nConsider the transformation $\\mathbf T$, given by\n$\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v))$,\nwhere $u=x+y$ and $v=y$. Find the representation of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane."] [17592186198243 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nx+y = u\n$$\n\nand $y = v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x + y)^{2} + y = 3⟹u^{2} + v = 3⟹v = 3 - u^{2}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y = v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\ny =-1⟹v =-1\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{v = 3 - u^{2}; v =-1⟹{u = ± 2; v =-1\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2, -1)$.\n\n![](images/84/84c624aab456b9a090c9c4e6d23c82d8e1f69425050b6f3ee9d4afd18e3f0cec.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D) = \\big\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: -2 \\leq u \\leq 2, \\quad -1 \\leq v \\leq 3-u^2 \\big\\}\n$$"] [17592186199011 :question/problem "Consider the region $D$, given by\n$D = {(x, y): 1 \\le x + y \\le 2, 1 \\le x - y \\le 3}$\nand the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$,\nwhere $u = x + y$ and $v = x - y + 1$. Find the representation of $T^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane."] [17592186199011 :question/answer-fields 17592186197906] [17592186092078 :question/problem "![](images/e8/e8ff769df4089e6ed9a47d56d7317acf41b8aef6a255abc705c0e7ec31a446ea.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n![](images/a2/a274b413d20b9aada9f32bc789557097df1a643805c2e8b12c389e3832352c95.png)"] [17592186092079 :question/problem "A triangle $D$ has vertices $(1,1)$, $(4,5),$ and $(-2,1).$ What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n![](images/5c/5cb4d5976a3e7153d7041711ab449a1f3af48000021be1d1c4a2c321cbec3467.png)"] [17592186092906 :question/problem "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$?"] [17592186092906 :question/answer-fields 17592186091606] [17592186064022 :question/problem "Each side of a square is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/min}$. How fast is the area of the square decreasing when the sides are $10\\,\\text{cm}$?\n\n![](images/a0/a06eb0963dc6d01e45acb9349e5232ba70a11c98c99ee63c2ec0dae3d748f5ed.png)"] [17592186064022 :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA = x^2\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(A) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(x^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/min}$, so we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} = -2\n$$\n\nAlso, when the sides are $10\\,\\text{cm}$, we have $x=10$. Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= (-2) \\cdot 2(10) =-40cm^{2}/min\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square decreases at a rate of $40\\,\\text{cm}^2/\\text{min}$."] [17592186064023 :question/problem "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $20 \\text{cm}^2\\text{/s}$. If $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square respectively, then find an expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds."] [17592186064023 :question/worked-solution "This situation is different. Here, the *area* is changing at a constant rate, and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/f4/f4125cebd7553bb8b106a8baff39a6e4a120b9d9e5cdf2c4c9c0a9a676558897.png)\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA = x^2\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(A) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(x^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= -20cm[math]^2[/math]/s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-20 &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot 2x \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= -\\frac{10}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186064024 :question/problem "![](images/26/2677121139896da73eca5277c7c23f193467001eabba976cb48b2232f4bcbad6.png)\n\nIn a testing facility, a scientist is increasing the diagonals of a simple square at a rate of $12 \\text{mm}\\text{/min}$. At what rate, in squared millimeters per minute, is the area of the square increasing when the length of the diagonal is $6\\, \\text{mm}$?"] [17592186064024 :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(A) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{h^{2}}{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= \\frac{dh}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dh}(\\frac{h^{2}}{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= \\frac{dh}{dt} \\cdot h\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ increases at a rate of $12\\,\\textrm{mm/min}$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dh}{dt} &= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=6$. Substituting this information into our expression for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}A}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= 12 \\cdot 6 = 72mm^{2}/min\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186064025 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}\n$$\n\nUsing implicit differentiation and the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(A) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{h^{2}}{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= \\frac{dh}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dh}(\\frac{h^{2}}{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= \\frac{dh}{dt} \\cdot h\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 2\\dfrac{\\textrm d h}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\n, so replacing\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm dt}\n$$\n\nin the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\frac{dh}{dt} &= h\\frac{dh}{dt} \\\\\n2\\frac{dh}{dt} &= h\\frac{dh}{dt} \\\\\n2 &= h\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at that particular moment, we have $h=2$, and therefore the area of the square at that moment is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{2^2}{2} = 2\n$$"] [17592186054983 :question/problem "If $X = {m, n}$, then $P(X) =$"] [17592186054983 :question/answer-fields 17592186051398] [17592186054987 :question/problem "If $C = {{2}, 3}$, then $P(C) =$"] [17592186054987 :question/answer-fields 17592186051422] [17592186087546 :question/problem "Write the complex number ![](images/c9/c93c5bf19eefeb4da980b81d46c4df10b3ff10aca21937aed885ef4111b1c9a7.png), shown in the following Argand diagram, in exponential form.\n\n![](images/33/335311049419928acc946721242c6e8e4096313dca1290b89b1cc7a2c30748fd.png)"] [17592186087546 :question/worked-solution "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n![](images/5d/5d6456ae3c5f168a14e761b44085defd76cd2292e8fa8d9b0eedf2cb55d97563.png)\n\nTo write it in the form ![](images/0e/0eb9bfc5066fce3fe0434f4dedf05cea2fff1e2fe38b32d4223eaf177ca2b654.png) where ![](images/d3/d3e8106a9b65dceed9a9c4e6fbc6341c0268517854b72f6e34fc3ec80117b915.png) and ![](images/60/6017ce5c562b2c1a2d1a47371c54820b04852fb851056aa171f88c411d92bd45.png) we first calculate ![](images/e3/e3862c77b505ea48e95acf4d632fa692c866c65079a2ac6b17543fd54e1c20ca.png)\n![](images/d0/d0bd5d7fc7f1d7b487fd940af95b5abb7e2fb134c02a55dbf9eb7d191abccca8.png)\n\nNow, since ![](images/c9/c93c5bf19eefeb4da980b81d46c4df10b3ff10aca21937aed885ef4111b1c9a7.png) lies in the second quadrant, we can find the angle ![](images/f4/f49e0503f6588c93dd1fd274c63f60c06b0eb77d5dce2d12dba750107139f124.png) as\n![](images/96/96e40550e19a81ffb655cd359242d8b0a37b1dde12e442252f976397a09a9760.png)\n\nto three decimal places.\n\nFinally, substituting ![](images/5d/5dcf5f8d9a0eb6d36f76152105bcb5a9af61a34bc99719183df2044d7f113edb.png) and ![](images/11/11dd78699cf02b8b10f1bd6dc78ee987d26954fc8b51a2e8327c2f24b5552458.png) into ![](images/9e/9ea3d5e030449e3b1da133b0aa004c6d26702021bf66e5672abe2bdd2c69eb9b.png) we get \n![](images/dd/dd0d831b250bbf985783cbb745a23c709a481ba08018395c683c3e2d4b00284a.png)"] [17592186060410 :question/problem "Consider the set $X = {a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h}$. Which subset must be placed into the blank in the set below so that it represents a partition of $X$?\n\n${{a, b}, {d, e}, {f, h}, ____}$"] [17592186060410 :question/answer-fields 17592186059052] [17592186222028 :question/problem "$f(x)∼\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}\\cos (\\frac{nπ}{2}x) + b_{n}\\sin (\\frac{nπ}{2}x)$\nLet $F(x) = 3x^{2} + 2x$ for $x ∈ [- 2, 2)$. The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of $a_{0}$.$a_{0} =$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186222028 :question/answer-fields 17592186220821] [17592186128842 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$u = \\dfrac{y^2}{x}, \\qquad v= xy$,\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n$x = {y^2}, \\qquad x=\\dfrac{y^2}3, \\qquad y=\\dfrac2x, \\qquad y=\\dfrac4x$."] [17592186128842 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\n$$\n\nis the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$.\n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu = \\dfrac{y^2}{x}, \\qquad v= xy\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1}$, that is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta$, which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$.\n\nWe substitute\n\n$$\nu= \\dfrac{y^2}{x}\n$$\n\ninto the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x = y^{2} & ⟹ & \\frac{y^{2}}{x} = 1 & ⟹ & u = 1 \\\\ x = \\frac{y^{2}}{3} & ⟹ & \\frac{y^{2}}{x} = 3 & ⟹ & u = 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v = xy$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y = \\frac{2}{x} & ⟹ & xy = 2 & ⟹ & v = 2 \\\\ y = \\frac{4}{x} & ⟹ & xy = 4 & ⟹ & v = 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: 1 \\leq u \\leq 3, \\quad 2 \\leq v \\leq 4\\big\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/12/1207d8cdb8afd7c55d712caca043edfe9f0d0395343a6cbd329cf5861ec3bce7.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(u, v))/(∂(x, y)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{y^{2}}{x^{2}} & \\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ y & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3y^{2}}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = -\\dfrac{x}{3y^2} = -\\dfrac{1}{3u}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0\n$$\n\neverywhere inside $\\Delta$. In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta$.\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)dxdy &= ∬_(Δ)\\begin{vmatrix}(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) & dudv \\\\ = ∬_(Δ) & -\\frac{1}{3u}\\end{vmatrix}dudv \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}dudv \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}du\\int_{2}^{4}dv \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot (\\ln 3 - \\ln 1) \\cdot (4 - 2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}\\ln 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186128843 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves.\n$e^y = \\dfrac{5}{e^x}, \\qquad e^y=\\dfrac{10}{e^x}, \\qquad e^x=e^y, \\qquad e^x=2e^y$"] [17592186128843 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\n$$\n\nis the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$.\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\ne^{x+y} = 5, \\qquad e^{x+y}=10, \\qquad e^{x-y} = 1, \\qquad e^{x-y} = 2\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu = e^{x+y}, \\qquad v = e^{x-y}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1}$, that is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $\\Delta$, which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$.\n\nWe substitute\n\n$$\nu=e^{x+y}\n$$\n\ninto the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\ne^{x + y} = 5⟹u = 5; e^{x + y} = 10⟹u = 10\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v =e^{x-y}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\ne^{x - y} = 1⟹v = 1; e^{x - y} = 2⟹v = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: 5 \\leq u \\leq 10, \\quad 1 \\leq v \\leq 2\\big\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/10/105745a82e8f67b086f526b0166e3b442d3eaf02e4df69314ed9d3ffd5fca071.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(u, v))/(∂(x, y)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}e^{x + y} & e^{x + y} \\\\ e^{x - y} & -e^{x - y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-e^{2x} - e^{2x} \\\\\n&=-2e^{2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-2x}=-\\dfrac{1}{2uv}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)}\\neq 0\n$$\n\neverywhere inside $\\Delta$. In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta$.\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)dxdy &= ∬_(Δ)\\begin{vmatrix}(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) & dudv \\\\ = ∬_(Δ) & -\\frac{1}{2uv}\\end{vmatrix}dudv \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∬_(Δ)\\frac{1}{uv}dudv \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{5}^{10}\\frac{1}{u}du\\int_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{v}dv \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (\\ln 10 - \\ln 5) \\cdot (\\ln 2 - \\ln 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\ln 2)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129021 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$u =3x-2y, \\quad v=x+y$,\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(3x-2y)^2 - y - x = 0$ and the line $x+y=4$."] [17592186129021 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\n$$\n\nis the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$.\n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu = 3x-2y, \\qquad v=x+y\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1}$, that is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta$, which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$.\n\nSubstituting $3x-2y = u$ and\n\n$$\nx+y = v\n$$\n\ninto the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(3x - 2y)^{2} - y - x = 0⟹u^{2} - v = 0⟹v = u^{2}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting\n\n$$\nx + y = v\n$$\n\ninto the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\ny + x = 4⟹v = 4\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{v = u^{2}; v = 4⟹{u = ± 2; v = 4\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2, 4)$.\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: -2 \\leq u \\leq 2, \\, u^2 \\leq v \\leq 4\\big\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/0a/0a3d0211dfbe18e5737b62e8b61cf9308af2735ef827d14bae7b5dbfffaa4faa.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(u, v))/(∂(x, y)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = (5)^{-1} = \\dfrac 15\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0\n$$\n\neverywhere inside $\\Delta$. In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta$.\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)dxdy &= ∬_(Δ) \\mid (∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) \\mid dudv \\\\\n&= ∬_(Δ)\\frac{1}{5}dudv \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}\\int_{-2}^{2}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{4}dv]du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}\\int_{-2}^{2}[v]_{u^{2}}^{4}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}\\int_{-2}^{2}(4 - u^{2})du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5} \\cdot 2\\int_{0}^{2}(4 - u^{2})du \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5}[4u - \\frac{u^{3}}{3}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5}(8 - \\frac{8}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5}(\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{32}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129516 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the following lines.\n$3y = 5x + 2, 3y = 5x - 2, 2x = y, 4x = 2y + 3$"] [17592186129516 :question/answer-fields 17592186126566] [17592186129884 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$u = x + y, v = x - y$,\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x + y)^{4} - x + y = 0$ and the line $y = 0$."] [17592186129884 :question/answer-fields 17592186128755] [17592186179434 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v+7 \\\\ u+v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $\\mathbf T$ maps the endpoints of $\\overline{PQ}$ to the endpoints of $\\overline{P'Q'}.$ So, to find the image of $\\overline{PQ},$ we find the images of each endpoint.\n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing the endpoints of our segment:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $\\mathbf b$ in each column:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe image of $\\mathbf u$ under $\\mathbf T$ is given by $A\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b.$ So, by applying $\\mathbf T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}7 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -10 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}7 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the endpoints of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(0,-2)$ and $(-3,1).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/61/61508357c514f50b27498bee50dce36d22b7f55f0baf8c2d6a58ad2d53dc879d.png)"] [17592186179435 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that we can express the given transformation as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v+1 \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $\\mathbf T$ maps the boundary of $\\Delta$ to the boundary of the image. So, to find the image of $\\Delta,$ we find the images of each side.\n\n![](images/69/69d92e24d5b0c81e54d3878793efe7c07d16fc963222997fe4fec4ffb3f93bd5.png)\n\n          \n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our square:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $\\mathbf b$ in each column:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe image of $\\mathbf u$ under $T$ is given by $A\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b.$ So, by applying $\\mathbf T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertices of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(1,-2), (3,0), (1,2),$ and $(-1,0).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/dd/ddd14bdebe21c830396e044721811cf6b9c7bd7db067e4d0ff7d35e02a592894.png)\n\n          \n\nNotice that since $\\det(A) = 2 >    0,$ this transformation is orientation-preserving."] [17592186179436 :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx = v - u-6, \\qquad y = v -2u  .\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\n- $\\det(A) = 1$\n\n- The transformation $\\mathbf T$ is orientation-preserving"] [17592186179436 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}v-u-6 \\\\ v-2u\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-u+v \\\\ -2u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T$ is $[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $\\det(A) > 0,$ the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ is orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [17592186179440 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3u+4 \\\\ 4v-2u\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3u \\\\ -2u+4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T$ is $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|\\det(A)| = |12| =12.\n$$"] [17592186180093 :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$, defined as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?"] [17592186180093 :question/answer-fields 17592186178197] [17592186092014 :question/worked-solution "Given a geometric series, the sum of the first $N$ terms is given by the formula\n\n$$\nS_N = a_1\\dfrac{(1-r^N)}{1-r}\n$$\n\nwhere $a_1$ is the first term of the series and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nIn our case, $a_1 = 3$. To find the value of $r$, we find the ratio of the first two terms in the series:\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_2}{a_1} = \\dfrac{6}{3} = 2\n$$\n\nIf we substitute $a_1 = 3$, $N = 7$, and $r = 2$ into our formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{7} &= 3 \\cdot \\frac{1 - 2^{7}}{1 - 2} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot \\frac{1 - 2^{7}}{-1} \\\\\n&=-3(-127) \\\\\n&= 381\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186092015 :question/worked-solution "Given a geometric series, the sum of the first $N$ terms is given by the formula\n\n$$\nS_N = a_1\\dfrac{(1-r^N)}{1-r}\n$$\n\nwhere $a_1$ is the first term of the series and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nIn our case, $a_1 = 2$. To find the value of $r$, we find the ratio of the first two terms in the series:\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_2}{a_1} = \\dfrac{-8}{2} = -4\n$$\n\nIf we substitute $a_1 = 2$ and $r = -4$ in the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{6} &= 2 \\cdot (1 - (-4)^{6})/(1 - (-4)) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\frac{1 - 4^{6}}{1 + 4} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\frac{-4095}{5} \\\\\n&=-1638\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186092016 :question/problem "The second term of a geometric series is $20$ and the third term is $100$. What is the sum of the first $6$ terms?"] [17592186092016 :question/worked-solution "The sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\nS_N = a_1\\dfrac{(1-r^N)}{1-r}\n$$\n\nwhere $a_1$ is the first term of the series, and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nTo find $r$, we find the ratio of the consecutive terms $a_2$ and $a_3$:\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_3}{a_2} = \\dfrac{100}{20} = 5\n$$\n\nNow we must determine the value of $a_1$. To do this, we use the formula for the $n$th term, which states that\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1}\n$$\n\nFor $n = 2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{2} &= a_{1} \\cdot 5^{2 - 1} \\\\\n20 &= a_{1} \\cdot 5 \\\\\na_{1} &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if we substitute $a_1 = 4$, $N = 6$, and $r = 5$ into the formula for $S_N$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{6} &= 4 \\cdot \\frac{1 - 5^{6}}{1 - 5} \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot \\frac{1 - 5^{6}}{-4} \\\\\n&= 5^{6} - 1 \\\\\n&= 15624\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186092021 :question/problem "What is the sum of the geometric series $1 + 6 + 36 + \\cdots + 46\\,656$?\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that $46\\,656 = 6^6$.*"] [17592186092021 :question/worked-solution "The sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\nS_N = a_1\\dfrac{(1-r^N)}{1-r}\n$$\n\nwhere $a_1$ is the first term of this series, and $r$ is the common ratio. In our case, we have $a_1 = 1$.\n\nTo find $r$, we find the ratio of the consecutive terms $a_1$ and $a_2$:\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_2}{a_1} = \\dfrac{6}{1} = 6\n$$\n\nNow we must determine $N$ (the index of the last term). To do this, we use the fact that\n\n$$\na_N = a_1 \\cdot r^{N-1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the hint, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n46656 &= 1 \\cdot 6^{N - 1} \\\\\n46656 &= 6^{N - 1} \\\\\n6^{6} &= 6^{N - 1} \\\\\n6 &= N - 1 \\\\\nN &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if we substitute $a_1 = 1$, $r = 6$, and $N = 7$ in the formula for $S_N$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{7} &= 1 \\cdot \\frac{1 - 6^{7}}{1 - 6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{6^{7} - 1}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{279935}{5} \\\\\n&= 55987\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186092700 :question/problem "The second term of a geometric series is $\\frac{1}{12}$ and the third term is $\\frac{1}{36}$. What is the sum of the first $6$ terms?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186092700 :question/answer-fields 17592186090370]]